The two structures under the maps, and what they share.

### Swiss table (`hashmap`, `set`)

- `control_group.hpp`: the group of control bytes, one per slot, matched together:
  `vector_group.hpp` on x86-64 (16 bytes, one SSE2 compare and pmovmskb through `ca::simd`),
  `swar_group.hpp` elsewhere and in an integer-only build (8 bytes in a 64-bit word, portable,
  no vector unit needed). A full
  slot holds the low 7 bits of its key's hash; EMPTY and DELETED have the high bit set.
  `match(tag)` returns the slots whose tag is equal, so a lookup compares a key only on a tag hit,
  about 1 in 128 slots. Both give the same interface (`WIDTH`, `SHIFT`, `bits`, `first`, `from`),
  so the table is written once. `empty_control` is one shared group of EMPTY bytes that every
  table without storage points at, so lookups need no branch for the empty case.
- `hash_table.hpp`: the table itself, over slots of any type: one block holding the control bytes
  then the slots, power-of-two capacity, at most 7/8 full, triangular probing over aligned groups.
  `hashmap` stores `item<K, V>` in the slots, `set` stores K.

### B-tree (`map`)

`btree_node.hpp`: nodes of up to 12 sorted keys and their values in two separate arrays, so a scan
inside a node touches keys only, plus the parent and the index in it; one spare slot lets an
insertion overflow a full node before it splits. `btree_branch` adds the children: child i holds
the keys between keys i - 1 and i. `btree_iterator` walks in key order through parent links,
showing each entry through a projection (`item<const K&, V&>`, a key, or a value).

### Shared

- `key.hpp`: `lookup_of<K>`, the type keys are looked up by (`K::view_type` when K has one, else
  `const K&`); `key_order` (`compare()` when available, else `<`); `make_from`, which builds a key
  only when an insertion needs one, by constructor, `clone()` or `K::make`; `clean`, which runs a
  key's or value's `cleanup()` keeping the first error.
- `range.hpp`: a begin and an end for range-for, what `keys()`, `values()` and `items()` return.
